<h2>Tracks and commentary</h2>
Here are some song things. It's hard to write about stuff I've
written.  Very weird.<p>

<ul>
<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Night.ogg">Night</a>:  Disciples of Beckett frequently contend that we're 
all waiting for something.  This may be so, but some manage
to wait more gracefully than others.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Trash.ogg">Trash Entertainment</a>:  Most really good books, plays or games 
have a lot of distractions throughout that keep you from 
focusing on the main point, objective, or message.  Life 
follows this pattern so closely that it's difficult to even 
formulate a guess.   One can only surmise that whatever God
is, it does its job well.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Leave_It_Behind.ogg">Leave it Behind</a>:  Waking, you discover that you're not the
person you were a minute before.  Half asleep, you realize that
you aren't the same person you were last year, last week, yesterday,
or fifteen minutes from now.  You may be enlightened, but the you 
thirty seconds away won't be.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_No_Regret.ogg">No Regret</a>:  Sometimes it's more important to take any action
than to make sure you take the right one.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Almost_Alright.ogg">Almost Alright</a>:  Introspection eventually breaks down.  You 
have to live with your decisions, you don't always need to 
justify them.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Whats_the_Plan.ogg">What's the Plan?</a>:  Tom Glynn plays drums for this track, 
as well as on Trash, Night, Leave it Behind and No Regret.    <p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Goat.ogg">Goat</a>: Inspired by an early cyberpunk novel.  I think fear of 
identity is one of the most rational impulses a person can
have, but is escape really possible?  Even the Buddha ended up
bequeathing us a hundred thousand statues of a human form that 
he insisted was meaningless.  <p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Opportunity_Fades.ogg">Opportunity Fades</a>:  This is almost less a song than a 
collection of sounds.  Reich hears music in the background 
noise of daily life.  For me, this is close to the 
reverse.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Juggernaut.ogg">Juggernaut</a>:  A song about group consciousness and the degree
to which it compromises free will.   Really. <p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Jesus.ogg">Jesus</a>:  Recorded Live in Ancestor Square, St. George Utah.  
Ryan Anderson joined us on Drums.   If you truly believe that
admittance into heaven will be granted on the basis of the 
correct syllables whispered to the guy with the flaming sword,
here's a thought:  suppose you get three guesses and your first
guess is wrong.<p>

<li><a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Ocean.ogg">Ocean</a>:  Also recorded live in Ancestor Square.  My guitar 
pedal died going into the solo, but the crowd carried us.
Interpreting your own dreams is either dishonest or dangerous.
I have nothing to say about this song.
tify them.<p>
</ul>

(No text for '<a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Truth.ogg">Truth</a>' or '<a href="music/Mazer_Rackham_-_Sorrow_Me.ogg">Sorrow Me</a>')<p>

